The user invoked `{{command_prefix}}{{args_placeholder}}`. Treat
`{{args_placeholder}}` as the subcommand and its flags.

Available subcommands: {{subcommand_list}}. If it is empty or is not one of
these, say so, list them, and stop.

There are two kinds of subcommand, and they are not run the same way.

**CLI-backed** — `install`, `update`, `init`, `check`, `explain`, `verdicts`, `gate`, `probe`, `seo`. Run the
matching command with `{{scripts_path}}`, passing the flags through unchanged,
then do the work below for that subcommand. Read the command's full output —
findings are ordered by severity, not position, so `head`, `tail`, `grep` and
`jq` drop the ones that matter.

**Agent procedures** — `decide`, `spec`, `mockup`, `make`, `critique`. There is no binary. Do not try to run one:
the section below **is** the command. A CLI can check what these produce; it
cannot do their work, because the work is judgment and authorship.

`decide` runs once per project. The other four run for each page, feature or
functionality, one at a time — never the whole product at once:

```text
decide     project-wide decisions, and the reason for each      once per project

spec       what exactly is being built: its smallest useful version, at every screen size
mockup     low-fidelity design of that spec, reviewed before any code
make       high-fidelity: the actual page or feature, built from the spec & mockup
critique   scrutinises what was built against the rules, its spec & mockup
```

## init

**Settle the surfaces before you run it.** Mode is the most consequential thing
`init` writes and the thing it cannot choose: with `--yes` it has nobody to ask, so
it declares no surface mapping at all, and every mode-gated rule stays silent until
someone does. A mode written into `{{config_file}}` then outranks your own reading
of the project from then on (`{{rules_path}}/01-modes.md` rule 1), so it has to be
chosen deliberately — and density is expensive to reverse.

You are the half that can fix this, because you are talking to someone who knows
the answer and the CLI is not. So, first:

1. **Ask what the product is**, in their words rather than Jig's — a marketing
   or content site, a signed-in application, an internal tool people use all
   day? A project usually has more than one of these, and each is a surface.
2. **Map each to a mode** using the table in `{{rules_path}}/01-modes.md`:
   `editorial` for first-visit content, `product` for the signed-in app,
   `operator` for dense daily tools. State the mapping in one line and let them
   correct it.
3. **Write `jig.config.json`** with those surfaces before running anything.
   `init` reads an existing config and honours it — writing one mode file per
   declared mode — so this is how a decision reaches the tokens.
4. **Then run the command.**

Where there is genuinely one surface, say so and move on; the point is that the
mode was chosen rather than defaulted into.

**Surfaces declared after `init` do not reach the page until `init` runs again.**
Writing a mode into `{{config_file}}` changes what agents read; the token files the
page imports still carry the mode `init` wrote. Run `{{scripts_path}} init` again after
any change to `surfaces`, and `check` will say when the two disagree.

Afterwards, report what it detected, the brand colour it derived and where that
came from, and the surfaces it used — the output says whether they came from
`jig.config.json` or from the default.

If the token layer does not exist yet, this is the command that creates it. **Do
not author token values yourself.** `init` derives the brand colour, checks it
against the contrast floor in both light and dark, and records a checksum for
each file it writes; a file you write by hand is untracked, so `jig update` can
never refresh it again. `jig check` now reads the token layer back and holds it
to those floors either way, so a hand-written value will be caught — but caught
late, and unmaintainable, is worse than derived.

## check

The CLI decides the rules a machine can decide. **It is half the review**, and
its own attestation says `judgment=not-run` to make that explicit.

Do the other half yourself:

1. Load `rules.index.json` and filter it to the entries marked `pass: code`.
   **That list is the work.** Do not decide from the filenames which rules are
   likely to matter and read only those — the rule you would not have thought to
   open is the one you are about to break. Read whatever files those ids live in.
2. Return a verdict for **every id on the list**: `ok`, `finding`, or `n/a` with
   one line of reason. `n/a, no form on this page` is a verdict; silence is not.
   Leave the `pass: screen` rules alone: they need the rendered composition,
   which is not in any file you have, and guessing at them here is how this
   command and `critique` come to give one page two verdicts.
   `{{command_prefix}}critique` owns those.

   **This is self-review, and it is the weakest arm in the system.** You built
   these files; you know what you meant, and what you meant is invisible in the
   artifact. It runs here because it is better than nothing and it is cheap. It
   does not replace `{{command_prefix}}critique`, whose arm C reads the same
   rules against the same source with an agent that has never seen this
   conversation. When the two disagree, the one that did not build the page is
   the one to believe.
3. Merge both halves into **one** report keyed by rule id, ordered by severity —
   not two lists. A reader should not have to know which half found what.
4. Run the self-check `L-04` at the end of `{{rules_path}}/00-anti-patterns.md`.

Then emit the attestation with both halves filled in:

```text
JIG_CHECK: version=<version> mode=<mode> mechanical=<pass|fail|skipped>:<n> warnings=<n> judgment=<ran|skipped>:<n> files=<n> styled=<n>
```

Take `mechanical=`, `warnings=`, `files=` and `styled=` from the CLI's own line.
`warnings=` counts the findings `check` reported as warnings. `mechanical=pass`
means no errors, and nothing more: a page that is not usable on a phone can
carry `pass:0` with several warnings, because the mobile detectors warn rather
than fail CI. A record that says `pass` with warnings above zero is not a clean
page — say what the warnings were.

If `check` could not run, that is
`mechanical=skipped:0` — never `pass`, which would report a clean result for a
check that inspected nothing.

`judgment=`'s `<n>` is **the number of ids you returned a verdict for**, not the
number of findings. It exists because `ran` on its own cannot be checked: an
agent that read four rules and an agent that walked all of them both wrote
`ran`. A number that is far below the size of the `pass: code` list is a
sampled review, and it says so without anyone having to ask.

`judgment=ran` here means the `code` rules ran. It does not mean the page was
reviewed — nothing in this command looks at a rendered page, so a screen whose
stylesheet never loaded passes every part of it. Say so if the user is treating
a clean `check` as a finished review, and point them at
`{{command_prefix}}critique`.

## install

Report where the skill landed and at which scope. If it warned that a global
install already exists, do not work around it — that warning is the system
refusing to leave two contradictory skills for one harness.

**The Stop hook is the user's to accept, not yours.** In Claude Code at project
scope, `--hook` adds an entry to `.claude/settings.json` that runs `{{scripts_path}}
gate` when an agent tries to finish, and holds it there while the files it changed
fail `check` or the step it just ran is unfinished. Run without the flag it is not
added, and `--no-hook` says so outright. Do not pass `--hook` on the user's behalf:
tell them it exists, say plainly that it can stop an agent finishing, and let them
answer. A hook you chose for them is a change to their editor they did not make.

## explain

`--layer` is the entry point for an agent that knows what it is trying to do but
not the rule number. `explain --layer` names the six and the question each
answers; `explain --layer layout` lists that one. The layers are a view, not a
location — nothing was renumbered to fit one, so `P-02 Button` sits in
`components` and `P-04 Form` in `patterns` while both keep the `P-` address they
have always had.

Print the CLI's output as it stands. It is already the rule's full text — do not
summarise it, and do not paraphrase the correction into your own words: the
wording is the rule.

**It takes a word as well as an id.** `explain contrast` searches every title
and body and lists what matches; one match prints in full. Use it whenever you
are about to reason about an area of the system rather than a specific finding
— before reviewing colour, before writing motion — instead of guessing which
rules apply or reading a whole rule file to find out. `explain <letter> --list`
prints one section, `explain --list` prints every id.

Reach for this before paraphrasing the rules from memory. A rule you half-recall
is the failure mode this command exists to prevent.

If the id is a `P-` or `M-` spec, the output says it is a specification rather
than a rule, with no ❌/✅ pair and no detector. That is correct, not a gap.

A well-formed id that does not exist errors, naming every id in that section —
offer the nearest one rather than guessing what the user meant. A word that
matches nothing errors differently, suggesting `--list`; that is a search with
no hits, not a typo, so widen the word rather than inventing an id.

## update

**Run this one as `{{update_path}} update`, not at the pinned version.** Every
other subcommand uses the pin so the CLI and these rules agree; `update` exists
to move that pin forward, so pinned it refreshes to the version already
installed and reports success for a no-op — "Updated Jig → <same version>" —
and nobody ever upgrades.

Report what moved and what was left alone. Files reported as skipped were edited
locally and are the user's; never re-apply Jig's version over them.

---

Everything in `{{rules_path}}/` is yours to read. Cite rules by id, and cite any
rule you deliberately break with the reason, in one line.

## spec

Say exactly what is being built — a page, a feature, or a piece of
functionality — before any of it is designed. This command writes no markup and no
CSS.

**Start with what it is for, not how it is laid out.** "A page where the user
creates an invoice" is a spec. "A dashboard with a sidebar" is a layout with
nothing in it yet: a sidebar, a container width and a navigation structure are
guesses until there is something real to put in them.

### Refuse rather than guess

Stop and say which of these is missing, rather than proceeding:

- `{{config_file}}` does not exist → run `init` first. Without it there is no
  mode and no token layer, so there is nothing for a spec to be specific to.
- No `DECISIONS.md` with substance → run `{{command_prefix}}decide` first. A spec
  implements the project's decisions, and cannot check itself against ones that
  do not exist.
- The user named nothing → ask which page, feature or functionality.
  `{{command_prefix}}spec` with no argument is not a request to invent one.

Read `DECISIONS.md` before asking anything. Do not re-ask what it already records;
cite it instead. If the screen touches an item in its **Unresolved** section, ask the user
about it by name; do not settle it in the spec.

### 0. If the page already exists, describe it before you change anything

An existing page is specified from what is there, not redesigned on the way past.
Read the page and its stylesheet, render it at each size the spec will name, and
write the frontmatter from what you find: the regions in their real order, the
hierarchy as built, the navigation at each width. Then ask the user the questions
below only where the page does not answer them — what it is for, who arrives, what
is deferred — and show them the description to confirm or correct.

Two things this is not. It is not a redesign: a region you would have arranged
differently is still recorded as it is, and `critique` is where that argument
belongs, with the rules behind it. And it is not a rubber stamp: a page that
contradicts `DECISIONS.md` is described accurately and the contradiction noted in
the prose, because the spec is a record of what is, and the review is what says
whether that is right.

**Asked to redesign it instead, run this command normally.** The difference is
what the old page counts as: its content is the brief — the copy, the real data,
the questions its FAQ answers — and its structure is not. Decide the composition
from the rules and the answers, not from what the markup happens to do now, or
the redesign is the same page in new colours. Anything that genuinely must
survive is a constraint and belongs in the spec by name: a URL linked from
elsewhere, a field order the back end depends on, wording somebody signed off.
Ask which of those exist; do not assume the whole page is one.

The commands after this run unchanged.

### 1. Ask, two or three questions at a time

**This is a required interaction, not a suggestion.** Ask two or three questions,
then **wait**. Do not send a questionnaire. Do not write the spec and present it
for approval on your first response — a spec synthesised from a one-line prompt
and handed over for a yes is the failure this command exists to prevent, and it
looks exactly like the command working.

Ask about **structure**, not appearance. Colour, type scale and density were
settled at `init` and live in `{{config_file}}` and the token layer; re-deciding
them per screen creates a second source of truth for the values Jig exists to
centralise. If the user raises appearance, record it and move on.

**Every question carries an example answer**, for the same reason `decide`'s do: a
question someone has to decode gets a worse answer than one they can react to, and
the example shows the shape. Say it is an example, not a suggestion.

- Round 1 — what is it for? What does the user need to do there ("create an
  invoice")? Who arrives, and what is the single most important thing they must be
  able to see or do?
  > *For example: "comprehension first, sign-up second. They arrive from the
  > product page having already decided to try it, so no explainer above the
  > cards. The most important thing is the limits — the price matters equally and
  > must never dominate."*
- **Then scope it — be a pessimist.** Of everything this could include,
  what is the smallest version that is useful on its own? Ask that plainly and
  expect to cut. "Comments with attachments, mentions, reactions, editing,
  threading and notifications" is a wishlist; "text and a submit button" might be
  V1. What is cut goes in `later:`, by name — deferred on the record, not
  forgotten. Everything after this designs V1 only.
- Round 2 — what content and states does it carry? Empty, loading, error,
  first-run, and the realistic range (0 items, 5, 500)?
  > *For example: "three plans, a monthly/annual choice, four FAQ questions, a
  > footer. The billing choice is the only state — the page is static, nothing
  > loads, nothing fails."*
- Round 3 — only what is still unresolved: entry points, where it leads, what
  must not happen here.
  > *For example: "they arrive from the product page or a docs search, and every
  > plan goes straight to sign-up. Nothing on this page may ask for a sales
  > call."*

**Ask about the phone by name.** What does the reader see first on a phone, and
what moves, stacks, or goes behind a control?
> *For example: "the plans come first on the phone" — that is the answer this
> question wants, and everything else about the narrow composition is the
> system's to settle.* If the conversation only ever
describes the wide screen, the phone composition will be derived from it rather
than designed — and a derived phone composition is a squeezed desktop.

### 2. Run `L-01`

Read `L-01 · Layout method` in `{{rules_path}}/03-patterns.md` and run its five
steps against the answers. The spec's structural half **is** that output — group,
order by importance, space from the inside out, align to a grid, and state the
hierarchy plainly enough that the squint question can be answered against it.

### 3. Write it down — one composition per screen size

`.jig/specs/<surface-slug>.spec.md`. Frontmatter carries what can be compared to
a built page; prose carries the reasoning that no check can read.

**Write a whole composition for every size, starting with the phone.** A spec
with one set of regions and a note about what changes when it is narrow is one
spec doing the work of three. It is always the wide composition that gets
designed and the narrow one that gets derived, which is how a page arrives on a
phone as a squeezed desktop.

```yaml
---
feature: sign in to an existing account      # the task, not the screen
surface: login page
mode: product              # from {{config_file}} — state it, never leave it implied
confirmed: false           # only the user sets this to true; see below
sizes:                     # phone first. Each size is a whole composition, not a diff.
  phone:                   # judged at 360px
    regions:               # in order, top to bottom
      - brand: wordmark only
      - form: the single primary task
      - recovery: secondary, below the action
    hierarchy: [form, brand, recovery]    # most important first
    grouping: { form: proximity, recovery: continuity }
    grid: { columns: 4 }
    nav: none on this screen
  tablet:                  # judged at 768px
    same-as: phone
    why: "one centred form; a second column would hold nothing"
  desktop:                 # judged at 1280px
    regions:
      - brand: left column — wordmark and one line on what the product does
      - form: right column, the single primary task
      - recovery: below the action, in the form column
    hierarchy: [form, brand, recovery]
    grouping: { form: proximity, brand: common region }
    grid: { columns: 12, form: 5, brand: 6 }
    nav: none on this screen
  wide:                    # judged at 1600px — where a layout with no upper bound shows itself
    same-as: desktop
    why: "content is capped at 1200px, so 1600 adds margin and nothing else"
states: [default, loading, error, success]
decisions: [The Engineer Reads First]     # every DECISIONS.md entry this screen implements, by name
later: [social sign-in, remember this device]   # cut from V1, by name — the next specs start here
indexable: true            # from the mode — editorial yes, product/operator no. Say why when you override it.
title: "Pricing — Hoistline"          # ≤ 60 characters, the search result's first line
description: "Three plans, every price visible. No sales call."   # ≤ 155
mockup: pending            # `mockup` sets approved or skipped, from the user's own words
mockup_at:                 # where the approved drawing is: a .jig/mockups path, or a Figma or Stitch link
deviations: []             # `make` writes here; `spec` leaves it empty
---
```

- **`phone` is written first, and in full.** It is the most common screen, and the
  one a derived composition fails on worst.
- **`indexable:`, `title:` and `description:` are copy, decided here.** The mode
  sets the default — `editorial` is first-visit content and is indexable,
  `product` and `operator` are what somebody reaches after signing in and are
  not — and a page overrides it with a reason: a CV shared by link, a
  confirmation page, a page that exists for one recipient. A page that is not
  indexable needs no title or description here and must carry `noindex` when
  built (`J-123`).
- **`wide` is required too, and is where an unbounded layout shows itself.** A
  page judged only to 1280 never reveals that its content has no cap: at 1600 a
  three-column grid becomes four thin ones, a header's logo and nav drift apart,
  and a line of prose runs past its measure (`B-11`). `same-as: desktop` with a
  `why:` is the right answer for a page that caps its content — and saying so is
  the point.
- **`landscape` is optional, judged at 900px.** Write it only when the
  composition genuinely changes between tablet and desktop — a navigation that
  expands there, a grid that goes three to two. Most pages do not, and a size
  written to be written costs a composition, a frame, a render and a row of
  verdicts on every page afterwards.
- **`tablet` and `desktop` are written in full too.** `same-as: <size>` is allowed
  when nothing changes, but only with `why:`. It is a claim that the composition
  holds at that width — `critique` checks it on a render — not a way to leave the
  size out.
- **The widths are where a composition is judged, not where breakpoints must
  fall.** The CSS breaks wherever the content needs it, and there may be more
  breakpoints than sizes. A navigation row, for example, breaks at the width where
  its labels fit.
- **No shell before there are features.** If the product has no navigation yet,
  `nav:` says `none yet` — it is decided once there is more than one feature to
  move between, not invented for the first one.
- **Regions say what the content is, not what it looks like.** `- plans: three
  cards` describes a shape; `- plans: a comparable set, one per tier` describes
  content, and the second is what `make` can choose an element from. `H-119` runs
  its decision order against these lines, so a region named only by its
  appearance leaves the element to chance.
- **`nav:` at every size, on any screen with navigation — decided by `P-14`'s table
  at that width, not copied.** It is the field most likely to be written once and
  copied, and most likely to be genuinely different at every size on a page that
  works. Count the destinations and run the table for each size: five links fit on
  one row at 768px and 1280px, so there the value is the links in a row, and the
  Menu button exists only at the widths where they do not fit. In a live run all
  four specs wrote "menu button, top right" at phone, tablet **and** desktop, citing
  a `DECISIONS.md` entry that said only where the button sits. Every page then hid
  five links behind a menu on a 1280px screen, and every later step faithfully
  built and approved it.

Every frontmatter field must be answerable by looking at the built page at that
size. If a field cannot be, it is prose — put it below the frontmatter, where it
belongs. "Feels focused" is prose. "Three regions, form first" is a field.

Add fields the surface needs (`form: { steps, validation }` for a form). Do not
add fields nothing can check.

### 3b. Check it against the decisions before anyone confirms it

A live run produced a spec that ranked the price above the plan limits, on a
product whose confirmed `DECISIONS.md` said the limits are the headline and the
price never dominates. The agent had written that decision itself, one command
earlier. Nothing in the chain would have caught it: `critique` compares the page
to the spec, never the spec to the decisions.

- List every `DECISIONS.md` entry the screen implements in `decisions:`, by name.
- Check **every size's** fields against each one. A field that contradicts a
  decision is fixed now, before confirmation. It is not a deviation — deviations
  record what building revealed, not what writing got wrong.
- **A menu button where the links fit is refused.** Before confirmation, read `nav:`
  at every size. If it says a menu button at a width where `P-14`'s table says the
  destinations fit, fix it now. A decision about where the menu button sits is
  about position, not about whether a button exists at that width — it does not
  override `P-14`.
- **Every reference must resolve.** A decision is cited by its name; a rule by an
  id that `{{command_prefix}}explain` returns. Where nothing decides something,
  write `unspecified — make chooses one it can defend, and records it`. Never write
  a sentence that implies a rule exists when it does not. The same run wrote
  "FAQ arranged per DECISIONS.md rules" where no such rule existed — and the next
  agent would have gone looking for it, found nothing, and invented one.
- **"The design system decides" is not a value.** When the user defers — "you decide",
  "whatever Jig says", "leave it to the system" — that is an instruction to look the
  answer up, not a phrase to copy into the spec. Search in this order: the `P-`
  pattern for the component (`{{command_prefix}}explain <component>`), `L-01`'s five
  steps, then the rules. Write the composition that lookup gives — "stacked, Pro
  first", "Menu button, links in a vertical list" — and cite the id it came from.
  Three of six specs in a live run wrote "arrangement decided by design system" into
  the frontmatter, and the build that read it had nothing to build from.
- **Before asking for confirmation, read every field back and refuse any value that
  names nobody.** "Decided by the design system", "as appropriate", "per best
  practice", "TBD", "standard layout" each hand the decision to no one. Replace each
  with the looked-up composition. Only when the lookup genuinely finds nothing does
  the field say `unspecified — make chooses one it can defend, and records it`; and
  say in the confirmation message which fields those are, so the user can decide
  them instead.

### 4. Get it confirmed

Show the spec and ask the user to confirm it. Then **stop**.

`confirmed: true` records that **the user said so in their own response**. You
may not set it on their behalf, and you may not treat your own summary,
`{{config_file}}`, or the absence of objection as confirmation. An unconfirmed
spec is not a weaker spec — it is not a spec, and `make` will refuse it.

If the user changes something, revise and ask again. The run is incomplete until
they confirm.

## mockup

**Low-fidelity design.** Draw the confirmed spec in grayscale, at every size it
names, and get it looked at **before** any code is written. Once approved, the
drawing is what `make` builds to and what `critique` checks against, alongside the
spec. The spec stays the durable record, and everything the review changes goes
back into it first, so the two never disagree.

It draws **what one spec defines** — its V1 — and nothing deferred to `later:`.
Never the whole product.

### Refuse rather than guess

- No `.jig/specs/<surface>.spec.md` → run `{{command_prefix}}spec` first.
- `confirmed: false` → nobody has agreed to what you would be drawing. Ask, and stop.
- The request is "mock up the app" or "the whole site" → say that a mockup draws
  one specified page, feature or functionality, and offer to run `spec` for it.

### 1. Ask the user how they want it drawn

**Ask every time, before drawing anything.** Three ways, and the choice is the
user's:

1. **HTML** — you draw it as `.jig/mockups/<surface-slug>.html`. Needs nothing
   connected. `.jig/` is outside what `check` scans and outside what ships, which
   is where a working drawing belongs.
2. **Figma** — drawn on a Figma canvas through Figma's MCP server.
3. **Google Stitch** — generated through Stitch's MCP server.

Jig ships neither tool and writes nothing for either. They are MCP servers the user
connects to their own agent, and the user decides whether to.

**If they choose Figma or Stitch, check that its server is among your tools.** If it
is not, tell them plainly: that tool's MCP server is not connected to this agent;
they need to connect it — Figma's through its official MCP server, Stitch's with a
Stitch API key — and some agents only see a newly added server after a restart.
Then **stop and wait**. When they say it is connected, check your tools again
before drawing. Do not switch to HTML on their behalf; offer it, and let them
choose.

- **Drawing in Figma.** One page named for the feature, one frame per size at the
  spec's widths — 360, 768, 1280, 1600 — phone first. Grey fills and strokes only; no
  library components, styles or variables from the product's design system, for
  the same reason the HTML carries no tokens.
- **Drawing in Stitch.** Ask for a low-fidelity grayscale wireframe of this one
  feature, at each size, with the spec's regions in order and its real labels.
  Stitch leans towards finished, coloured screens: if what comes back is polished
  — brand colour, imagery, decoration — it is not a mockup. Ask Stitch again for a
  wireframe. If it still will not produce one, tell the user and ask whether to
  switch to HTML. Do not review a finished-looking screen as though it were one.

  **One screen per size, one request each:** `deviceType` `MOBILE` for 360, `TABLET`
  for 768, `DESKTOP` for 1280. A live run asked once per project and got phone
  screens only.

  **A timeout is not a failure.** Generating a screen takes minutes, longer than the
  call waits, so the call often times out while Stitch keeps working and saves the
  screen afterwards. In a live run all three agents took the timeout as "Stitch made
  nothing", retried — which made a duplicate — and switched tools, while their
  screens were sitting in the project. So, when the call times out or drops:
  1. **Do not call it again.** A second request is a second screen.
  2. List the project's screens every 30 seconds or so, for up to 10 minutes. The
     list of projects does not show screens; only listing one project's screens
     does.
  3. When the screen appears, carry on with it. If the project already holds a
     screen for that size from an earlier attempt, use it rather than generating
     another.
  4. Only when 10 minutes pass with nothing, tell the user, and ask whether to try
     again or switch to HTML or Figma. Do not switch on their behalf.

  Before review, confirm there is a screen for **each** size, not only the phone.
- **An existing design the user already has** for this feature, in either tool, can
  stand in for drawing one. Read it through the server, and compare its structure
  to the spec at every size. It will usually be high fidelity, so say plainly that
  this step reviews structure only, and do not take up its colours or type as
  decisions — those belong to the tokens and `DECISIONS.md`.

The rules below apply whichever of the three it is. They are written for HTML; in
Figma or Stitch they apply to the frames.

### 2. Draw it — structure only

**Low fidelity means low detail.** The purpose is to judge structure before
appearance, and appearance wins every time it is allowed in: a reviewer shown
finished colour and type stops evaluating the layout and starts reacting to the
look. So the drawing contains:

- **Grayscale only.** No brand colour, no accent, no status colours — unless colour
  *is* the feature (a colour picker, a status legend), and then only there.
- **No tokens and no project CSS.** Use the wireframe stylesheet below, pasted into
  the file's `<style>`. The mockup must not look like the product; if it does, it
  has become a design review.
- **No icons, imagery, shadows or decoration.** An image is a labelled box that says
  what goes there. An icon is its name in brackets: `[search]`.
- **Real words, not lorem ipsum.** Labels, headings and button text are part of the
  structure — "Create invoice" versus "Submit" is a decision worth reviewing.
- **Every size the spec names, as its own frame** — phone, then tablet, then desktop, then wide,
  stacked top to bottom, each drawn with its own markup. Size is structure, not
  detail: a phone composition that is only the desktop one narrower is the failure
  specs are written per size to prevent. A `same-as:` size is drawn anyway, so the
  claim can be seen to hold.
- **Navigation at every size, exactly as the spec's `nav:` says for that size.** On
  the phone that usually means the Menu button, drawn where `DECISIONS.md` puts it —
  and, because an open menu changes the structure, a second phone frame labelled
  `phone — menu open` showing the links as a vertical list and the button reading as
  close (`P-14`). A phone frame with no way to the other pages is not a simpler
  drawing; it is a missing region. A live run approved exactly that in Figma, and the
  build copied the gap.
- **The primary state, plus any state that changes the structure.** An empty state
  that replaces a table, or an error that moves a form, is drawn. The rest stay
  listed in the spec. Do not draw what building will reveal: 2,000 contacts, a
  thirty-character name, a slow network — unless the feature is about exactly that.

```html
<style>
  /* Jig wireframe. Structure only: do not add colour, fonts, shadows or images. */
  *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body { margin: 0; padding: 24px; font: 16px/1.5 system-ui, sans-serif; color: #222; background: #f4f4f4; }
  .size { margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 13px; color: #666; }
  .frame { background: #fff; border: 1px solid #999; margin: 0 0 40px; padding: 16px; overflow: hidden; }
  .frame[data-size="phone"] { width: 360px; }
  .frame[data-size="tablet"] { width: 768px; }
  .frame[data-size="desktop"] { width: 1280px; }
  .frame[data-size="wide"] { width: 1600px; }
  .frame[data-size="landscape"] { width: 900px; }
  .region { border: 1px dashed #aaa; padding: 12px; margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .region > .name { display: block; font-size: 12px; color: #777; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .media { background: #ddd; color: #555; display: grid; place-items: center; min-height: 120px; }
  .field { display: block; width: 100%; min-height: 40px; border: 1px solid #888; background: #fff; }
  button { font: inherit; padding: 8px 16px; border: 1px solid #555; background: #fff; }
  button.primary { background: #444; color: #fff; border-color: #444; }
  h1 { font-size: 28px; margin: 0 0 12px; }
  h2 { font-size: 22px; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  h3 { font-size: 18px; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .dims { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; color: #555; }
</style>
<script>
  // Jig wireframe. Labels every region with its measured size, e.g.
  // "navigation · 326 × 48". Measured from the drawing, never typed by hand.
  function jigDims() {
    document.querySelectorAll('.region > .name').forEach((label) => {
      const box = label.parentElement.getBoundingClientRect();
      let dims = label.querySelector('.dims');
      if (!dims) { dims = document.createElement('span'); dims.className = 'dims'; label.append(dims); }
      dims.textContent = ` · ${Math.round(box.width)} × ${Math.round(box.height)}`;
    });
  }
  addEventListener('load', jigDims);
  addEventListener('resize', jigDims);
</script>
```

Each frame is a `<section class="frame" data-size="phone">`, preceded by a
`<p class="size">` naming the size and its width. **Every region is labelled** —
navigation, header, each group, the primary action — with the name the spec uses,
in a `.name` label, so the drawing and the spec can be read against each other.

**Every label carries the region's dimensions in px**, width × height at that frame's
width: `navigation · 326 × 48`. In HTML the script above writes them; do not type
them yourself, because a number that was not measured is a guess the reviewer will
trust. In Figma, put the frame's own width and height in the layer's label. In
Stitch, ask for the labels in the prompt, and where the screen does not show them,
say so in review rather than supplying numbers.

**The dimensions describe the drawing; they are not the build's values.** A
navigation 326px wide at 360 is the phone width minus the frame's padding and border, not a width to
hard-code — `make` still sizes with tokens and fluid widths (`D-111`). What they are
for is review: is the header taking a sixth of the phone? Is a tap target under
`--size-touch-target`? Is the hero pushing the plans below the first screen?

### 3. Put it in front of the user

**Check the drawing against the spec before anyone sees it.** For each size, go
down the spec's `regions:` and its `nav:` and find each one in that frame. A region
the spec names and the frame lacks is drawn now, not raised in review. This matters
most on the phone, where a region dropped for space is easy to miss and the reviewer
is looking at what is there, not at what is not.

Tell the user where the drawing is — the file path, or the Figma or Stitch link —
and how to open it. If the harness can render —
a browse skill, Playwright, a headless browser — render each frame and show them the
images; a drawing nobody looked at has not been reviewed.

Then ask **structural** questions, not whether they like it: is the most important
thing the first thing seen at each size? Does anything belong in a different group?
Is anything here that V1 does not need?

### 4. Every change goes into the spec first

When the review moves, adds, cuts or regroups anything, **edit the spec**, then
redraw from it. Never adjust the drawing alone. A mockup changed in review and not in
the spec has quietly become the real specification, and `make` would build from a
document that no longer describes what was approved.

A request for more — "add reactions while you're at it" — goes to `later:`, unless
the user explicitly moves it into V1. Then the spec's scope changes, and it is
confirmed again.

### 5. Record the outcome in the user's words

- The user approves → set `mockup: approved` in the spec, and `confirmed: true` if
  review changed it. Record where the approved drawing is in `mockup_at:` — the
  file path, or the Figma or Stitch link. Only their own response counts; your summary of the drawing,
  or no objection, does not.
- The user says to skip it → `mockup: skipped — <their reason>`. For a change small
  enough that drawing it costs more than building it, that is a reasonable call; it
  is still theirs to make.

Then stop. The next step is `{{command_prefix}}make`.

## make

**High-fidelity.** Build the actual page or feature from its confirmed spec and its
approved mockup — the real tokens, components and copy, working.

**After a critique,** `make` is how its findings get fixed: read the report, fix
each finding, run the finish again, and then `critique` runs again. See step 5 of
`critique`.

### Refuse rather than guess

- No `.jig/specs/<surface>.spec.md` → say so and run `{{command_prefix}}spec`
  first. Do not write a quick spec of your own to satisfy the check; a spec you
  wrote and accepted in the same breath is the thing the confirmation exists to
  prevent.
- `confirmed: false` → the spec exists but nobody has agreed to it. Ask for
  confirmation and stop.
- `mockup: pending` → the feature has not been looked at. Run
  `{{command_prefix}}mockup`, or ask the user whether to skip it. Do not decide to
  skip it yourself.

### Build

**Build from the spec and the mockup, never by copying the mockup.** The spec says
what is being built; the approved mockup shows its structure at each size — what
comes first, what is grouped with what, what stacks on the phone. Match that
structure at high fidelity.

What you do not do is paste the mockup's HTML into the product, or take the code a
Figma or Stitch frame will generate for you. The drawing has fixed frame widths, grey
boxes and no tokens; pasted in, it carries none of the rules or tokens the product is
built on. Read it; do not copy it.

If the spec and the approved mockup disagree, the spec was not updated during review.
Stop and ask the user which is right, fix the spec, and continue — do not choose
between them yourself.

If `mockup: skipped`, build from the spec alone.

Build V1 only. Nothing in `later:`, however little extra it looks.

Build the **phone** composition first, then add what `tablet` and `desktop`
change. Write base styles for the phone and add width as it is available — not
the desktop layout with overrides that take it apart again. A layout built wide
and subtracted from ends up correct at exactly the widths somebody checked.

**Choose every element from what its content is, before styling any of it**
(`H-119`). For each region in the spec, run the decision order: what is this
content — a heading, a list, a sequence, a quotation, tabular data, navigation,
a control, a landmark? Is there a native element whose meaning is that? Does it
describe the content accurately? Only when nothing fits is a generic container
right. The page must read as what it is with the stylesheet off, in an order
that makes sense; CSS then arranges it, and never the reverse.

**Write the metadata in this change, not the next one** (`J-120`). The title,
description and preview text are copy: they come from the spec, they are written
by whoever wrote the headline, and they move when it moves. A page that is not
indexable carries `noindex` instead, from its own metadata rather than from
`robots.txt`, which is public and advisory and protects nothing.

Then the ordinary rules apply: tokens by semantic name, the relevant
`03-patterns.md` section for each component, `05-copy.md` for every string.

### Record what you changed

Building reveals that a spec was wrong somewhere — that is normal and expected.
**Silence is the failure, not the change.** Append to `deviations:`:

```yaml
deviations:
  - field: sizes.phone.form.steps
    from: 1
    to: 2
    why: "password reset needs an email round-trip; one screen cannot hold both"
```

A spec that quietly stops describing the page is worse than no spec: a later
review then validates the page against fiction and reports it clean.

### Finish

You are not done until all three of these hold. They are steps, not advice: in a
live run one build skipped `check` and shipped 53 errors, another offered 36 as
"non-blocking", and a third drew no phone navigation where its approved mockup had a
menu and reported "matched exactly".

**1. `check` passes.** Run `{{scripts_path}} check --all` and paste its
`JIG_CHECK:` line into your report, as printed. `mechanical=fail:<n>` means not done:
fix every error and run it again. No mechanical finding is non-blocking, and you do
not get to decide one is. Every warning is fixed, or waived on its own line with
`jig-allow <ID>: <why>` (see `gate`); an explanation in your report is not a waiver.
A report with no `JIG_CHECK:` line, or one you typed yourself, is not a finished
build.

**2. The build matches the approved mockup at each size.** Skip only when
`mockup: skipped`. Render the page at 360px, 768px, 1280px and 1600px — with a browser, not in
your head — and set each render beside the mockup at the same width. Go region by
region, in the mockup's order:

| Size | Mockup region | In the build? | Same place and order? |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| phone | header: name + Menu button | yes | yes |
| phone | plan cards, stacked | yes | no — Pro first |

Every **no** is either fixed now or appended to `deviations:` with a `why`. Never
write "matches" without the table: the claim is what the table is for. A control the
mockup draws — a Menu button, a toggle — is in the build only if it works: tap it.

**3. Each size's spec fields are accounted for.** Say which fields the built page
satisfies and which it does not — **at each size**. A page that satisfies `desktop`
and was never looked at on a phone has satisfied a third of its spec. Then run the
self-check at the end of `{{rules_path}}/00-anti-patterns.md`.

Report the `JIG_CHECK:` line, the table, and any new deviations. Then suggest
`{{command_prefix}}critique`.

## critique

Scrutinise what was built against the rules and its spec. This is **not** a second `check`, and the difference is
not effort — it is evidence.

`check` reads files. `critique` looks at the page **and** reads the source
against the rules `check` has no detector for. A hierarchy failure is in no
file: it is in what the files produce together, so no amount of careful reading
finds it. That is what the render arm is for.

Every judgment rule says which pass owns it, in `pass:`. The corpus divides
three ways, and each part needs a different instrument:

| | rules | instrument |
|---|---|---|
| mechanical, has a `detector` | a small minority | `check`, deterministic |
| judgment, `pass: screen` | the render | arm A, looking at the page |
| judgment, `pass: code` | **the largest group** | arm C, reading the source |

The third one is the reason this command exists in its current form. `check`
judges only what a detector can decide, which is a fraction of the corpus. Every
other `pass: code` rule is judgment, and until something reads the source against
it, it is enforced by nothing but the building agent having remembered it. An
agent under load does not remember 60-odd rules; it consults the handful it
thought to search for, and ships the rest unchecked.

No two arms judge the same rule, which is why they can never hand a user
competing verdicts on one id.

### Refuse rather than guess

- No `DECISIONS.md` with substance → run `{{command_prefix}}decide` first. `critique`
  judges the page against the project's decisions as well as the rules, and has
  nothing to judge that half against without them.
- The user named nothing → ask which page, feature or functionality.
- There is no `.jig/specs/<surface>.spec.md` → say so and continue **rules-only**,
  reporting `spec=missing`. Do not invent the spec the page should have had; a
  spec written after the page, from the page, agrees with it by construction.

### 1. Three assessments that cannot see each other

Run all three. **No arm may see another's output**, and none may see the
conversation that built the page. Running them in one head anchors them to each
other; do not shortcut it for cost, speed or context size.

- **A — the reader.** Delegate to a subagent. Give it the URL or file, the spec,
  the approved mockup (from `mockup_at:`), `DECISIONS.md`, and the corpus. It also
  judges every decision in `DECISIONS.md` against the page and writes
  `decisions.json` (step 1c). It judges the `pass: screen` rules against the render, **and the `P-` patterns the spec's regions use** — a navigation region means `P-14`. Those patterns are not in `rules.index.json`, so walking the index alone never reaches them. It writes its verdicts to `.jig/critique/<surface>/screen.json` (step 1c). Do
  **not** give it the build conversation. If it needs that conversation, it is
  not reviewing the page, it is agreeing with itself.
- **B — the machine.** Run `check`. Deterministic, same input same output. It
  judges the rules that carry a `detector`, and nothing else — its own
  attestation says `judgment=not-run`, and that is a true statement about the
  rest of the corpus, not a formality.
- **C — the source reader.** A second subagent, separate from A. Give it the
  source files, the spec, and the corpus, and no render. It judges every
  `pass: code` judgment rule — the group `check` cannot reach — and writes its
  verdicts to `.jig/critique/<surface>/code.json` (step 1c).

**If you cannot delegate an arm, it is skipped. You do not run it yourself.** No
subagent available, a subagent that cannot read the project's files — either way
the arm reports `skipped` with the reason, in words, in the report. A live run had
an agent build a page and then run the reader arm on it in its own head; every
verdict it wrote cited what the spec *intended* rather than what the page *did*,
and its one finding asked the builder to confirm it. A skipped arm is an honest
gap. A self-run arm is a false review that looks like a real one.

Each reader is a subagent for the same reason a writer does not proofread their
own copy. An agent that built the page knows what it meant, and what it meant is
invisible in the artifact — which is the one thing a reviewer must not have.

A and C are kept apart for a narrower reason: an arm that has seen the render
stops reading the source and starts confirming the picture. They answer to
different evidence and must not share it.

### 1a. Arms A and C walk the index. They do not search it.

**This is not a style preference. It is the difference between covering the
corpus and sampling it.**

Load `rules.index.json`, filter to the pass the arm owns, and return a verdict
for **every id in that list**: `ok`, `finding`, or `n/a` with one line of
reason. A rule that does not apply is still answered — `n/a, this page has no
form` is a verdict.

Searching the corpus is how a *builder* works, and it is the right method there:
it finds the rules you can already name. It cannot find the rule for the mistake
you do not know you are making, which is the only kind of rule worth writing
down. A pass driven by search returns what the agent thought to look for, and
reports nothing about everything else — indistinguishable, in the output, from a
clean page.

The builder cannot do this. It is holding a brief, a spec and a half-written
page, and it will drop most of the corpus under that load. An arm with one job
can walk all of it, which is the whole reason the work is split.

**Never narrow the list by guessing relevance before reading.** Filter by `pass`,
and by mode where a rule is mode-gated. Nothing else. An arm that decides in
advance which rules are worth checking has reintroduced search with extra
steps.

### 1b. What makes a verdict real

Every one of these was produced by a live run of this command, by arms that had
been told to walk the index and believed they were doing it.

**An `n/a` must name what the rule is about.** Not "does not apply" — *what* does
not apply. `n/a, D-27 is about optical alignment and this page has no icons` is a
verdict. `n/a, rule context unavailable` is an absence wearing a verdict's shape,
and the difference is mechanically checkable: run
`{{command_prefix}}explain <id>` and the rule either resolves or it does not.

One run returned `C-68 — rule not found in accessible corpus` and
`D-27 — rule context unavailable`. Both resolve instantly. A third described
`D-25` as being about label spacing; it is about proximity hierarchy. None of
the three had been read. `C-68` is *non-interactive elements styled like
interactive ones* — adjacent to the only real finding that arm had raised.

**An id you cannot resolve is a hard stop.** If `explain` fails on an id that is
in the index, that is a corpus or install defect. Say so, loudly, and stop.
Recording it as a rule that happens not to apply buries a broken install inside a
clean-looking report.

**Never treat the count as a target.** The instruction is *return a verdict for
every id*; `<n>` is the consequence. An arm asked to reach a number will reach
it — that is how the three fabrications above were produced, in one turn, after a
reviewer asked for a short pass to be completed. **A pass that comes back short
is re-run, not topped up.**

**An arm that contradicts itself is re-run, not merged.** A live run's reader
arm said, in one report, that a toggle hard-codes `60px`/`34px` instead of tokens
(`A-07`) and that nothing on the page is hard-coded (`H-47`). Two opposite verdicts
on the same file are not a disagreement to report; they are an instrument that
cannot be trusted, and every other verdict it returned is suspect with them.

### 1c. Verdicts go in files, and the CLI counts them

Each reader arm writes its own file. The arm reports back only that it wrote it.

```json
{
  "rendered": true,
  "artefacts": [".jig/critique/pricing/360.png", ".jig/critique/pricing/768.png", ".jig/critique/pricing/1280.png", ".jig/critique/pricing/1600.png"],
  "verdicts": [
    { "id": "D-115", "verdict": "ok", "reason": "no sideways scroll at 360, 768, 1280 or 1600" },
    { "id": "P-14", "verdict": "finding", "reason": "menu button at 360 does not open; aria-expanded never set" },
    { "id": "A-60", "verdict": "n/a", "reason": "A-60 is about competing icons; this page has none" }
  ]
}
```

`screen.json` carries `rendered` and `artefacts`; `code.json` needs only `verdicts`.

**And the project's own decisions, one verdict each**, in
`.jig/critique/<surface>/decisions.json`:

```json
{
  "verdicts": [
    { "decision": "Voice", "verdict": "finding", "reason": "headings and buttons are Title Case; the decision says lowercase" },
    { "decision": "Trial and onboarding", "verdict": "finding", "reason": "Team and Scale say \"start free trial\"; the trial was replaced by the free Solo plan" },
    { "decision": "Navigation", "verdict": "ok", "reason": "menu button top right at 360, five links at 768 and above" }
  ]
}
```

Every `##` and `###` heading in `DECISIONS.md` is a decision and gets exactly one
verdict, judged against the built page. `Unresolved` is not a decision. The rules
are the system's; these are the project's, and a page can satisfy all 115 rules
while breaking the file written to hold what this project chose. In a live round
one page shipped "start free trial" two commands after the owner had killed
trials, and another used Title Case against a lowercase decision. Both passed
every rule.

**A rendered review is measured, not only described.** At 360, 768, 1280 and 1600px, run the
probe in the browser and save what it returns beside the verdicts:

```
# If a browser is on this machine, one command renders and records every width:
{{scripts_path}} probe --run <page> --save <surface>

# A built site that links its styles from the site root (/assets/site.css) must be
# served, or nothing loads. Name the build directory and the CLI serves it:
{{scripts_path}} probe --run dist/pricing/index.html --serve dist --save <surface>

# Otherwise, at each width: open the page in whatever browser you have, evaluate
# what `{{scripts_path}} probe` prints, and pipe exactly what it returned into:
{{scripts_path}} probe --save <surface>
```

`--run` drives a headless Chrome, Chromium or Edge it finds for itself — a
project with Playwright or Puppeteer already has one — and needs nothing
installed. The Stop hook runs it too, before it judges a review, so a render is
not a step anyone can forget. Do not copy the build and rewrite its links to make
a file load: `--serve` shows the page a browser actually gets.

**The CLI writes the probe file, you do not.** `--save` reads the probe's output,
stamps it with the page's checksum, and stores it. That is what makes the file a
measurement: `verdicts` recomputes the checksum, so a file written by hand, or one
taken before the page's last edit, is rejected. A live run wrote a probe file itself
— five links and Escape closing a menu that has thirteen links and no Escape handler
— and its review passed on those numbers.

The probe opens the phone menu, presses Escape, measures sideways scroll, and reads
whether the styles and tokens actually applied. `verdicts` refuses `rendered: true`
without a probe at each width, and refuses an `ok` the probe contradicts — a `P-14`
"ok" on a menu that did not open, a `D-115` "ok" on a page that scrolls sideways. It
also fails a page that renders in the browser's default font, or shows `${` to
readers, whatever the verdicts say. Never write a probe file yourself: when it
disagrees with you, it is the review that is wrong.
Then run:

```
{{scripts_path}} verdicts <surface>
```

**It decides whether the review is complete, not you.** It fails on an id that is not
in the corpus, names every id in the arm's pass that has no verdict, rejects a rule
filed by the wrong arm or judged twice, rejects an `n/a` whose reason says the rule
was not read, requires `P-14` when the spec has navigation, and refuses
`rendered: true` without artefacts that exist. It ends with a `JIG_VERDICTS:` line.

When it fails, **re-run the arm it names**. Do not edit the file to make it pass, and
do not report an incomplete arm as having run. A live Haiku run of this command
before it existed produced invented rule ids, `screen=ran:1` filed as a review,
`code=ran:97` against 67, `ran:100` against both, and arms that reported to the
wrong agent entirely. Every one of those was forbidden here in prose. Prose did not
hold; a check does.

The merge reads the two files — not a summary an arm sent back. An arm's message can
go astray; its file cannot.

### 2. Look at the page

If a browser is available this is **required**, not optional. Use whatever the
harness provides — a browse skill, Playwright, a headless browser. Reading the
source and imagining the page is not a render.

- **Operate the page, don't only look at it.** At 360px, open the navigation
  menu: the links must appear, `aria-expanded` must change, and the control's label
  or icon must show that it now closes. Use the billing control, the FAQ, anything
  that changes state, and check it changes. A menu button that renders is not a menu
  that works: in a live run four of six pages shipped a phone menu that did not open,
  or no menu at all, and every review that only looked passed them.
- **Render at every size the spec names** — phone at 360px, tablet at 768px,
  desktop at 1280px, wide at 1600px — and judge each size's fields against its own render. A
  `same-as:` claim is checked like any other field: render that size and see
  whether the composition really does hold.
- At every size, measure `document.documentElement.scrollWidth` against
  `clientWidth`. Greater means the page scrolls sideways at that width, which is a
  finding however good the rest of it looks.
- **Judge the collapse, not only the sizes** (`L-01` step 6). Between one width
  and the next: did the column count drop rather than the columns shrink, did the
  reading order hold, did an emphasised item stay distinguishable once everything
  was in one column, did anything genuinely wider scroll inside itself rather
  than taking the page with it?
- Compare the sizes. Did the phone change the **composition**, or only the
  **dimensions**? Reordering, stacking, collapsing, a different control — that is
  composition. The same layout with smaller numbers is not.
- Check the page is styled at all. A stylesheet that 404s renders a page that
  passes every file-based check ever written.
- **Read what a stranger meets first** (`J-120`). Open the head: does the title
  still describe this page, does the description still say what the page now
  says, and would either have survived the last copy change? A page whose
  metadata contradicts its own headline is the commonest drift there is, because
  a copy pass reads pages and nobody reads the head. Run `{{scripts_path}} seo`
  for what one page cannot see: a route that says `noindex` and sits in the
  sitemap, two pages claiming the same title, a site with neither sitemap nor
  robots.
- **Read the markup as a document, not a layout** (`H-119`). For each region:
  does its element say what the content is? Would the page still read correctly
  with the stylesheet off, in this order? A row of links that is not a `<nav>`, a
  heading that is a styled `<div>`, steps in no particular element, a comparison
  of values that is not a table: each is a finding, and each names the element
  the content asks for. A `<div>` is right where nothing more specific is true;
  say so rather than inventing a fault.
- Run the squint test from `L-01` against the render, not the analogue: render
  each size once more with `filter: grayscale(1)` on the root, and check that the
  primary action, the headings and the groups still read in order without colour.
  Keep the grayscale screenshots with the others; they are artefacts too. The
  analogue exists for when there is no browser.

If no browser is available, say so in one line and use `L-01`'s analogue. Never
skip it silently, and never report a render you did not do.

### 3. Compare the page to its spec and its mockup

**The spec**, field by field: for each size, say whether the page rendered at that
size satisfies each frontmatter field.

**The mockup**, size by size: set each rendered size beside the approved drawing
at the same width and compare the structure — the regions and their order, what is
grouped with what, what stacks or moves on the phone. Not the colour, type or
polish: the mockup is grayscale and low-fidelity on purpose, so its appearance is
not a target.

A difference from either is a finding **unless** `deviations:` already explains it
— that is what the recorded deviation is for. A difference explained by neither is
the more serious finding, because it means the spec has quietly stopped describing
the page. If `mockup: skipped`, compare to the spec alone and say so.

### 4. Report

Merge both assessments into one report, keyed by rule id, ordered by severity.
Say explicitly where the two agree, what only the machine found, what only the
reader found, and which machine findings are false positives. **The disagreement
is information, not noise to smooth over.**

Say what is right as well as what is wrong — two or three things, and why they
work. A review that only lists faults tells the reader nothing about what to
preserve while fixing them.

Then the attestation:

```text
JIG_CRITIQUE: version=<version> mode=<mode> surface=<name> spec=<confirmed|missing|unconfirmed> mockup=<approved|skipped|missing> rendered=<yes|no> screen=<ran|skipped>:<n> code=<ran|skipped>:<n> decisions=<ran|incomplete|missing>:<n> mechanical=<pass|fail|skipped>:<n> warnings=<n>
```

**Take `screen=`, `code=`, `decisions=` and `rendered=` from `jig verdicts`, never from your own
count.** If it reported an arm incomplete, that arm is `skipped` here with the reason,
until it is re-run and passes.

Three counts because there are three arms, and each can fail independently.
`mechanical=` is `check`'s result and uses `check`'s own field name deliberately,
so the two records line up. `screen=` and `code=` are the two reader arms, and
their `<n>` is **the number of rules the arm returned a verdict for** — not the
number of findings. An arm that walked the index reports the size of the list it
walked; an arm reporting far fewer did not walk it, and the number is where that
shows.

`code=skipped:0` is the state this command was rebuilt to make visible. It used
to be unsayable: the largest group of rules in the corpus went unjudged and the
report looked complete.

`rendered=no` is not a detail. Without it a clean report reads as "this page is
good" when it can only mean "the files are good" — and a page whose stylesheet
never loaded satisfies every file-based check there is.

`rendered=yes` means **every size the spec names** was rendered. If only some
were, the report names which, and says what was not seen.

**`rendered=yes` requires an artefact.** A screenshot, a rendered dump, something
that exists because a browser ran. Reading the source and reasoning about how it
would look is the `L-01` analogue, and the analogue is `rendered=no`. A live run
of this command emitted `rendered=yes` on a machine with no browser installed and
no image written anywhere — turning an honest limitation into a false assurance,
on the one field a reader leans on hardest. If you did not see the page, say you
did not see the page.

### 5. Then the cycle goes round again

A critique with findings is not the end of the feature. It is the middle of it.
Designing every case in advance does not work; fixing a page you can use does. So:

1. Tell the user the findings, and that the next step is `{{command_prefix}}make`
   to fix them — not the next feature.
2. `make` fixes them, runs its finish again, and records any spec change in
   `deviations:`.
3. Run `{{command_prefix}}critique` again on the same surface.
4. Repeat until the report has no errors or warnings, or the user has accepted each
   one that is left **by id, in their own words** ("leave E-65 for now"). Your
   judgment that a finding is minor is not acceptance.

Only then suggest `{{command_prefix}}spec` for the next feature. Starting the next one
with findings open means the second feature is built on top of the first one's
problems, and nobody goes back for them.

## probe

Run `{{scripts_path}} probe`. It prints one JavaScript expression — the render probe —
for the critique's screen arm to evaluate in a browser at each width. See step 1c of
`critique`. It changes nothing on its own.

## seo

Run `{{scripts_path}} seo` and report what it says. It needs no `DECISIONS.md`,
no spec and no confirmation: it reads what the project already serves, so it is
safe to run on the first day and on somebody else's codebase. It audits the whole project,
which is what one file cannot: a route whose metadata says `noindex` sitting in
the sitemap, two pages claiming the same title, a sitemap that lists nothing or
lists paths instead of full URLs. It counts the pages it found, a dynamic route
once and an endpoint (`rss.xml.ts`, `[id].txt.ts`, `api/`) not at all, since it
serves no page. It also counts whether a sitemap and a robots file exist;
neither is a finding, since no rule asks for them and a project with no domain
yet cannot write an honest sitemap. Do not invent one to clear a count.

It is not a phase. The per-page half belongs where the copy is decided and
written — `spec` records whether a page is indexable and what it claims, `make`
writes the metadata in the same change as the headline, `check` enforces the
budgets — because a pass you run afterwards is a pass that gets skipped on the
day the copy changes, which is exactly how a live site ended up serving
positioning it had already dropped.

## gate

Not a command to run. In Claude Code, `install` adds a Stop hook that runs
`{{scripts_path}} gate` whenever you try to finish. If files you changed have a
`check` error or warning, or a critique's verdict files fail `verdicts`, it refuses
to let you stop and hands you the failures as the reason. **Fix what it names.** Do
not edit verdict files to satisfy it, and do not report the work as done while it is
blocking. It also refuses when a critique's verdict files changed after `critique`
wrote them, in a session that did not run `critique`: a fix is judged by the next
review, never by the builder editing the last one.

A warning you are sure is right as it stands, because the detector guessed and the
guess is wrong here, is waived on its own line or the line above, in the file's
comment syntax: `jig-allow <ID>: <why>`. The reason is what the user reads, so write
the case, not "intended". An error cannot be waived, a waiver with no reason waives
nothing, and `check` prints every waiver it honoured. Waiving a warning to get past
the gate, when the fix was available, is the failure the gate exists to catch. After three
refusals in one session it lets you stop; then tell the user plainly that the work is
not finished and what is still failing.

It exists because every "run check" and "run verdicts" step in these procedures was
skipped in a live run, and pages that did not render their styles were reported
clean.

## verdicts

Run `{{scripts_path}} verdicts <surface>`, passing the argument through, and report its
output unchanged — every `✗` line and the closing `JIG_VERDICTS:` line.

It exists so that `critique`'s counts are computed rather than written. Read its
errors as instructions to re-run the arm they name. It never fixes a verdict file,
and neither do you: a file edited until it passes records nothing about the page.

## decide

Write `DECISIONS.md` beside the token layer — `jig/DECISIONS.md` by default, or
wherever `brand` in `{{config_file}}` puts the token files.

`spec`, `mockup`, `make` and `critique` are blocked until this exists: `spec` and
`critique` refuse to start without it, and `mockup` and `make` need a confirmed spec.
Nothing else is blocked — `install`, `init`, `check`, `explain`, `seo`, `probe`
and `verdicts` all run without it. `seo` in particular is an audit of what is
already there, and a project with no decisions yet still has a sitemap that
either contradicts its pages or does not.
`init` comes first, because this file sits beside the token files `init` writes.

### What belongs in it, and what does not

The token layer already holds **values**, and `{{config_file}}` already holds
**mode**. Neither can hold a **reason**. This file is the reasons.

- ✅ *"Our accent arrives as a whole field or not at all — a full-bleed section,
  a solid button, a filled state. Never a 2px underline, never an icon tint.
  Its authority comes from arriving in quantity, rarely."*
- ❌ *"The accent is `#FF6347`."* — that is a token. Restating it here creates
  two places to change it, and they will disagree.
- ❌ *"Use 4.5:1 contrast."* — that is `C-19`, and it is true of every project.
  A universal rule copied into a project file is a rule that can rot locally.

The test: **could this be derived from the tokens, the mode, or a numbered rule?**
If yes, it is already written down somewhere better. If no, it is a decision, and
this is where it lives.

**`decide` is product-wide. `spec` is per screen.** Ask what holds on every
screen. What one screen contains, who arrives at it, what its FAQ asks, where
something sits on it — those are `spec`'s questions. Asking them here makes the
user answer them twice, and a live run did exactly that: one agent gathered a
page's FAQ and arrival paths under `decide`, the other under `spec`.

One kind of placement **is** a decision: something that must sit in the same
place on every screen, such as where the menu button lives. That is taste, not
something a rule can settle, and it belongs here once. Write it as where the thing
sits **when it appears** — "the Menu button, wherever it appears, sits top right" —
never as "a menu button on every page". Whether a menu button exists at a width is
`P-14`'s, decided per size in `spec`: at widths where the links fit there is none. A
live run recorded "Menu button: top right, same on every page", and all four specs
read it as a menu button at desktop width too. Placement on a single
screen is not — and do not ask the owner where an element should go on a screen
at all. If the design system has a position, it is in the rules; if it has none,
`spec` settles it.

### How to write it

**Interview, do not infer.** Two or three questions per round, then wait. What
you are looking for is the places where this project has already chosen
something, or will have to:

**Ask every question with an example answer attached.** A question someone has to
decode gets a worse answer than one they can react to, and the example shows the
shape you need — a sentence about this product, not an adjective. Say plainly
that it is an example, not a suggestion.

- Round 1 — what is this product, and who is it for in a sentence the team would
  recognise? What should it never look like? Name real products, not adjectives.
  > *For example: "hosted log search — you point it at your cluster and ask in
  > plain text. For a backend engineer who has tried grep over SSH. It must never
  > look like an enterprise console: no SKU matrix, no 'contact us' pricing.
  > Closer to Fly.io than to a Datadog dashboard."*
- Round 1b — **what the product is for, and what you give up for it.** This file
  is project-wide, so both answers are about the product, not a page. A page's
  own purpose is `spec`'s question, and it inherits its direction from here.
  - *"If this product works, what can someone do that they could not before?"*
    That is the north star: one outcome, in the product's own words, that every
    later argument is settled against. A design direction without one drifts
    screen by screen, each change defensible on its own.
    > *For example: "an engineer finds the line in their logs without learning a
    > query language" — not "be the best log tool", which no screen can serve or
    > fail. The test: could you look at a design and say whether it moves toward
    > that sentence?*
  - *"When two good options conflict here, which side do you take?"* Fast over
    complete, plain over clever, fewer choices over more, density over comfort.
    > *For example: "comprehension wins over persuasion — if the clearer page
    > converts worse, we ship the clearer page", or "recovery wins over speed:
    > every destructive action is undoable, even where that costs a step".*
    Jig's own tiebreakers settle the system's conflicts; this settles the
    project's, and without it the agent settles them alone. In a live run a
    decision about where the accent may appear collided with a component rule
    and the agent resolved it on its own, correctly as it happens, and recorded
    the collision as a deviation. One line here would have decided it.

  **Refuse an adjective, ask again.** "Bold", "human", "trustworthy" and
  "premium" cannot be checked against a page, and every decision in this file is
  now judged against one. Push for the form that can: what it is closer to, what
  it would rather be than, what it refuses.
- Round 1c — **the personality, asked through the four things that produce it.**
  Every interface has one whether it was chosen or not, and it is not a mood
  board: it is decided by type, colour, corners and language, each of which is a
  value this project is about to set. Ask about all four, one question each, and
  say what each answer will become.
  - **Type.** Serif reads classic or editorial; a rounded sans reads friendly; a
    neutral sans stays out of the way and lets everything else carry the
    personality. Which is this? *(Becomes `--font-text` and `--font-display`.)*
  - **Colour.** Which colour is this product's, and what does it mean here? The
    psychology matters less than the reason: blue is familiar and safe, gold
    reads expensive, pink reads unserious. *(Becomes `--color-brand`, and the
    contrast floors still apply — a palette that fails them is not a
    personality.)*
  - **Corners.** Square reads formal or technical, a small radius is neutral,
    a large one reads playful. One answer for the whole interface: mixing square
    and round in one screen looks worse than either. *(Becomes the `--radius-*`
    scale.)*
  - **Language.** Official and impersonal, or plain and conversational? Words
    appear in every screen, so this decides more of the personality than any
    single visual choice. *(Becomes a voice decision here, applied by
    `05-copy.md`.)*
  > *For example: "neutral sans — the type should disappear. One colour, a muted
  > olive, because it is the one thing on the page that is not a plan or a price.
  > Square corners; this is a tool, not a toy. Lowercase and technical: ingest,
  > retention, seat — plain words, undefined, no throat-clearing."*

  **When they have no gut feeling, ask what the reader already uses.** The sites
  this audience spends its day in set the expectation; matching that is cheaper
  than teaching a new one. Take the direction, never the design: a project that
  borrows from a direct competitor looks like a second-rate version of it, which
  is also `A-01`'s point about invented decoration seen from the other side.
- Round 2 — for each thing the tokens already set, is there a rule about *how* it
  is used? Where does the brand colour go, and where is it forbidden? What earns
  emphasis?
  > *For example: "the accent appears exactly twice per page, both clickable —
  > the primary action and the active side of a control. Never on a checkmark, a
  > limit number or a feature list. Emphasis is earned by the one thing the
  > reader came to do."*
- Round 3 — what has the team already argued about, or reversed? A decision with
  a history is the one most worth recording, because it is the one most likely to
  be re-made wrongly.
  > *For example: "we shipped a 'most popular' badge for six weeks. Conversion
  > rose slightly; wrong-plan signups and support tickets rose more. We pulled
  > it, and that covers the effect, not the widget — no raised card either."*

**All three rounds run.** Do not write the file after round 2 because the answers
look complete. Round 3 asks for the one kind of decision a user does not volunteer
unprompted — the ones with a history — and a live run skipped it for exactly that
reason. In the arm that did ask, round 3 is where the unresolved items surfaced.

**Round 3 also asks, by name, what is still undecided.** "Is there anything the team
has not settled yet — something still argued about, or waiting on someone?" Ask it
even when nothing so far suggests there is.
> *For example: "whether we publish a public status page. It is between showing
> uptime on the pricing page and not mentioning it at all, and it waits on
> whether support can staff it."* Whatever the answer names goes in the
**Unresolved** section below, not into a decision the user did not make. Four of six
files in a live run dropped the one open item the owner had named.

**Write each decision as a named rule with its reason attached**, in the project's
own vocabulary:

```markdown
### The Stamp Rule

The accent appears as a whole field or not at all. A full-bleed section, a solid
button, a filled active state — never a 2px underline, never a small icon tint,
never a gradient stop. Its authority comes from arriving in quantity, rarely.

**Why:** thinly spread, it reads as decoration and stops meaning anything. The
audit test: if a coloured element is smaller than a section band, it is probably
wrong.
```

A name gives the team something to cite in review. A reason lets a future agent
tell when the rule does not apply, which a bare instruction never can.

**The north star and the tiebreaker are decisions like any other**, written with
the same shape and judged against every page:

```markdown
### What this page is for

A reader leaves knowing which plan fits them and which limit they would hit
first. Nothing else on the page outranks that.

**Why:** they arrive from the product page already interested; the job is to
remove the last doubt, not to sell again.

### When two options conflict

Comprehension wins over persuasion. If a clearer page converts worse, we ship
the clearer page.

**Why:** the reader is an engineer who has been sold to badly before, and the
support cost of a wrong-plan signup outweighs the signup.
```

**The reason is the owner's, or it is not written.** Write the `Why:` the user gave,
in their words or close to them. If they gave none, ask for it. If they still give
none, write `**Why:** not given` — never a reason you supplied. A live run invented
reasons for two real reversals; an invented reason is worse than none, because the
next agent weighs it as the team's and applies the rule where the team never would.

**Record what is still open in its own section, at the end:**

```markdown
## Unresolved

- **Annual discount on the pricing page.** Undecided between showing it on the
  toggle and only at checkout; waiting on finance. Until settled, specs do not
  commit to either — they ask.
```

Each item says what is undecided, between what, and what it is waiting on. This
section is not a placeholder and does not fail the gate: an honest "not decided yet"
is information. `spec` reads it, and asks the user instead of choosing when a screen
touches an unresolved item. If round 3 surfaced nothing, write `None named by the
owner.` under the heading, so a reader can tell it was asked.

### Finish

**Before you show it, account for every answer.** List each thing the user said,
round by round, and the section of the file it went to — a named decision, its
`Why:`, or **Unresolved**. An answer that went nowhere is either added or shown to
the user as left out on purpose, with the reason. Then go the other way: every
`Why:` in the file must trace to something the user said; delete any that does not,
and ask. Include this mapping in your message, below the file.

Show it and ask the user to confirm it is right before you stop. Leave no
`[TODO]` markers — the gate treats them as an unwritten file, correctly, because
a placeholder decision is not a decision.
